    answer: "Very often, yes. San Bernardino has some of the oldest residential housing stock in the Inland Empire — homes from the 1950s, '60s, and '70s where older Whirlpool, GE, and Maytag appliances have been running for 15–25 years. These machines are mechanically simpler than modern units with fewer electronics to fail, and they're frequently worth repairing. A 20-year-old Whirlpool Direct Drive with a worn drive coupling costs $120–$140 to fix. A replacement is $700–$900. We tell you honestly which way the math goes after diagnosis."
  },
